.TH GLOB 3 "" "helios-unix" "Library functions"
.SH NAME
glob, globfree \- expand filename patterns
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <helios/unix/glob.h>
int glob(const char *pattern, int flags,
          int (*error_callback)(const char *, int), glob_t *result);
void globfree(glob_t *result);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Expand *, ?, bracket sets/ranges and backslash escapes in pathname components.
A leading dot must be matched explicitly. Results are sorted bytewise unless
GLOB_NOSORT is set; locale collation is not implemented.
.PP
Zero-initialize glob_t before first use. gl_pathc counts paths and gl_pathv
contains allocated strings followed by a NULL pointer. Release results with
globfree, including partial results after an error.
.TP
GLOB_ERR
Abort on directory errors. The optional callback receives path and errno;
a nonzero callback return also aborts.
.TP
GLOB_MARK
Append / to directory names.
.TP
GLOB_NOCHECK
Return the pattern itself when no match exists.
.TP
GLOB_DOOFFS
Reserve gl_offs initial NULL entries in gl_pathv; initialize gl_offs first.
.TP
GLOB_APPEND
Append to an existing result. Use the same offset convention on all calls.
.TP
GLOB_NOESCAPE
Treat backslash as an ordinary character.
.TP
GLOB_TILDE
Expand the current user's ~ using HOME; ~otheruser is unsupported.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 on success; GLOB_NOMATCH, GLOB_NOSPACE or GLOB_ABORTED on failure.
These are glob results, not errno values. Paths are limited to 1023 bytes
and traversal to 33 components; oversized patterns return GLOB_NOSPACE.
The implementation also bounds result/offset growth.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
#include <helios/unix/glob.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int list_sources(void)
{
    glob_t result = {0};
    size_t i;
    int error = glob("*.c", 0, 0, &result);
    if (!error)
        for (i = 0; i < result.gl_pathc; ++i)
            puts(result.gl_pathv[i]);
    globfree(&result);
    return error;
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
mkstemp(3), helios-unix(7)
